Control Statements:
Control statements are used to control the flow of execution of a program. They determine the order in which statements are executed.

Control statements are mainly classified into three types:
1. Conditional Statements/Decision-Making Statements
Conditional statements are used to execute a block of code based on a given condition.

Types:
1. if statement:
  if(condition){statement}
2. if-else statement:
  if(condition){statement}else{statement}
3. else-if ladder
  if(condition1){statement}else if(condition2){statement}else{statement}
4. Nested if statement
  if(condition1){ if(condition2){statement}}
5. switch statement
  switch (val):
  case val1:statement;break;
  case val2:statement;break;
  ...
  default:
  Statement;
  break;


2. Looping / Iterative Statements
Looping statements are used to execute a block of code repeatedly until a given condition becomes false.

Types:
1. for loop
2. while loop
3. do-while loop
4. Enhanced for loop (for-each)


3. Jumping Statements
Jumping statements are used to transfer the control of execution from one part of a program to another.

Types:
1. break – Terminates a loop or switch statement.
2. continue – Skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.
3. return – Terminates the current method and optionally returns a value.

Ternary Operator in Java
The Ternary Operator (?:) is a conditional operator used to make decisions in a single line. It is a shortcut for the if-else statement.
syntax:
variable = (condition) ? expression1 : expression2;




What is syntax?
Syntax is the set of rules and regulations used to write statements in a programming language correctly.
